=date&sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22Invest+Ophthalmol+Vis+Sci%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2024 Feb 1;65(2):33.\n\ndoi: 10.1167/iovs.65.2.33.\n\n# Spatial Analysis Reveals Vascular Changes in Retinal and Choroidal Vessel Perfusion in Intermediate AMD With Reticular Pseudodrusen\n\n[Judy Nam](/?term=Nam+J&cauthor_id=38386332)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"School of Optometry and Vision Science, University of New South Wales, Sydney, Australia.\")   [2](#full-view-affiliation-2 \"Centre for Eye Health, Sydney, Australia.\") , [Lisa Nivison-Smith](/?term=Nivison-Smith+L&cauthor_id=38386332)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"School of Optometry and Vision Science, University of New South Wales, Sydney, Australia.\")   [2](#full-view-affiliation-2 \"Centre for Eye Health, Sydney, Australia.\") , [Matt Trinh](/?term=Trinh+M&cauthor_id=38386332)  [1](#full-view-affiliation-1 \"School of Optometry and Vision Science, University of New South Wales, Sydney, Australia.\")   [2](#full-view-affiliation-2 \"Centre for Eye Health, Sydney, Australia.\")\n\nAffiliations Expand\n\n### Affiliations\n\n-   1 School of Optometry and Vision Science, University of New South Wales, Sydney, Australia.\n-   2 Centre for Eye Health, Sydney, Australia.\n\n-   PMID: **38386332**\n-   PMCID: [PMC10896234](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10896234/)\n-   DOI: [10.1167/iovs.65.2.33](https://doi.org/10.1167/iovs.65.2.33)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\n# Spatial Analysis Reveals Vascular Changes in Retinal and Choroidal Vessel Perfusion in Intermediate AMD With Reticular Pseudodrusen\n\nJudy Nam et al. Invest Ophthalmol Vis Sci. 2024.\n\nShow details\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat Abstract PubMed PMID\n\nInvest Ophthalmol Vis Sci\n\nActions\n\n-   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Invest+Ophthalmol+Vis+Sci%22%5Bjour%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n-   [Search in NLM Catalog](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/nlmcatalog?term=%22Invest+Ophthalmol+Vis+Sci%22%5BTitle+Abbreviation%5D)\n-   [Add to Search](#)\n\n. 2024 Feb 1;65(2):33.\n\ndoi: 10.1167/iovs.65.2.33.\n\n### Authors\n\n[Judy Nam](/?term=Nam+J&cauthor_id=38386332)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"School of Optometry and Vision Science, University of New South Wales, Sydney, Australia.\")   [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Centre for Eye Health, Sydney, Australia.\") , [Lisa Nivison-Smith](/?term=Nivison-Smith+L&cauthor_id=38386332)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"School of Optometry and Vision Science, University of New South Wales, Sydney, Australia.\")   [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Centre for Eye Health, Sydney, Australia.\") , [Matt Trinh](/?term=Trinh+M&cauthor_id=38386332)  [1](#short-view-affiliation-1 \"School of Optometry and Vision Science, University of New South Wales, Sydney, Australia.\")   [2](#short-view-affiliation-2 \"Centre for Eye Health, Sydney, Australia.\")\n\n### Affiliations\n\n-   1 School of Optometry and Vision Science, University of New South Wales, Sydney, Australia.\n-   2 Centre for Eye Health, Sydney, Australia.\n\n-   PMID: **38386332**\n-   PMCID: [PMC10896234](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10896234/)\n-   DOI: [10.1167/iovs.65.2.33](https://doi.org/10.1167/iovs.65.2.33)\n\nItem in Clipboard\n\nFull text links Cite\n\nDisplay options\n\nDisplay options\n\nFormat AbstractPubMedPMID\n\n## Abstract\n\n**Purpose:** To examine the effect of reticular pseudodrusen (RPD) on retinal and choroidal vessel perfusion (VP) topography in intermediate age-related macular degeneration (iAMD) using refined spatial analyses.\n\n**Methods:** This was a retrospective cross-sectional study of 120 individuals with 30 iAMDRPD, 60 iAMDno\\_RPD, and 30 normal eyes, propensity-score matched by age, sex, and presence of cardiovascular-related disease. VP of the superficial and deep retinal and choriocapillaris vascular slabs was assessed from 6 × 6-mm optical coherence tomography angiography (OCTA) scans divided into 126 × 126 grids, with adjustment for various person- and eye-level factors. Grid-wise VP differences (%) among the groups were spatially assessed according to analyses based on the Early Treatment for Diabetic Retinopathy Study (ETDRS), eccentricity (µm), and degree (°).\n\n**Results:** VP was significantly decreased between iAMDRPD and iAMDno\\_RPD, across all vascular slabs in various ETDRS sectors (up to -2.16%; 95% confidence interval, -2.99 to -1.34; P < 0.05). Eccentricity analyses revealed more complex patterns: a bisegmented relationship where VP in iAMDRPD eyes decreased linearly toward 1000 µm then returned toward similar values as iAMDno\\_RPD, plateauing around 2000 µm in the superficial and 3000 µm in the deep retina (R2 = 0.57-0.9; P < 0.001). Degree-based analysis further showed that the greatest VP differences in iAMDRPD eyes were commonly located superiorly and nasally across all vascular slabs (P < 0.05).\n\n**Conclusions:** RPD appears to compound the vascular impact of iAMD, displaying complex spatial patterns beyond the ETDRS sectors. This highlights the importance of considering spatial delineations for future work regarding the role of RPD and vascular dysfunction.\n\n[PubMed Disclaimer](/disclaimer/)\n\n## Conflict of interest statement\n\nDisclosure: J. Nam, None; L. Nivison-Smith, None; M. Trinh, None\n\n## Figures\n\n [![Figure 1.](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b2a1/10896234/62e924f15fdb/iovs-65-2-33-f001.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b2a1/10896234/4f75a2177339/iovs-65-2-33-f001.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 1.\n\n**\n\n( **A** ) Raw OCTA…\n\n**\n\nFigure 1.\n\n**\n\n( **A** ) Raw OCTA images were processed (binarized, skeletonized, and adjusted for…\n\n**Figure 1.**\n\n(**A**) Raw OCTA images were processed (binarized, skeletonized, and adjusted for projection and shadowing artifacts) and then divided into 126 × 126 grids for calculation of gridwise VP. (**B**) Gridwise VP values were adjusted for covariables and compared among study groups. (**C**) Gridwise VP differences were assessed globally and spatially according to ETDRS sectors, and then eccentricity was assessed per 50 µm from the fovea and degrees per 1° around the macula. Topography maps were assigned a color scale, where *darker blue* represents decreasing VP, *darker green* represents increasing VP, and *white* represents unchanged VP.\n\n [![Figure 2.](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b2a1/10896234/73d811f0485b/iovs-65-2-33-f002.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b2a1/10896234/86be1db29170/iovs-65-2-33-f002.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 2.\n\n**\n\nGlobal analyses of VP differences…\n\n**\n\nFigure 2.\n\n**\n\nGlobal analyses of VP differences between iAMD RPD and iAMD no\\_RPD eyes and…\n\n**Figure 2.**\n\nGlobal analyses of VP differences between iAMDRPD and iAMDno\\_RPD eyes and between iAMDRPD and normal eyes in the superficial retinal slab, deep retinal slab, and choriocapillaris.\n\n [![Figure 3.](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b2a1/10896234/d4ea318c0de7/iovs-65-2-33-f003.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b2a1/10896234/d8679677f70c/iovs-65-2-33-f003.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 3.\n\n**\n\nSpatial analyses of VP differences…\n\n**\n\nFigure 3.\n\n**\n\nSpatial analyses of VP differences between iAMD RPD and iAMD no\\_RPD eyes in…\n\n**Figure 3.**\n\nSpatial analyses of VP differences between iAMDRPD and iAMDno\\_RPD eyes in the superficial retinal slab. Gridwise VP differences (%) were plotted as a topographical map (**A**) and assessed according to the ETDRS sectors (**B**), eccentricity from the fovea (**C**), and degrees around the macula (from −180° temporal to 0° nasal to 180° temporal) (**D**). Significance is denoted by \\**P* < 0.05 and \\*\\*\\**P* < 0.001. In **A**, *blue grids* represent decreased VP and *green grids* represent increased VP. The thick *red* and *tan lines* in **C** represent the fitted curve, and light *red dots* represent the 50-µm incremental eccentricity \\[95% CI\\] data. The thick *magenta line* in **D** represents adjoining average (95% CI) data; significant multiple comparisons with VP greatest decrease (*purple circle*) and VP least decrease (*aqua circle*) are circled; raw values are available in Supplementary Table S1.\n\n [![Figure 4.](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b2a1/10896234/04748849c888/iovs-65-2-33-f004.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b2a1/10896234/cb02a101b8a8/iovs-65-2-33-f004.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 4.\n\n**\n\nSpatial analyses of VP differences…\n\n**\n\nFigure 4.\n\n**\n\nSpatial analyses of VP differences between iAMD RPD and iAMD no\\_RPD eyes in…\n\n**Figure 4.**\n\nSpatial analyses of VP differences between iAMDRPD and iAMDno\\_RPD eyes in the deep retinal slab. Gridwise VP differences (%) were plotted as a topographical map (**A**), and they were assessed according to the ETDRS sectors (**B**), eccentricity from the fovea (**C**), and degrees around the macula (from −180° temporal to 0° nasal to 180° temporal) (**D**). Significance is denoted by \\**P* < 0.05, \\*\\**P* < 0.01, and \\*\\*\\**P* < 0.001.\n\n [![Figure 5.](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b2a1/10896234/1c5230584698/iovs-65-2-33-f005.gif)](https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/b2a1/10896234/5709286a0ed9/iovs-65-2-33-f005.jpg) \n\n**\n\nFigure 5.\n\n**\n\nSpatial analyses of VP differences…\n\n**\n\nFigure 5.\n\n**\n\nSpatial analyses of VP differences between iAMD RPD and iAMD no\\_RPD eyes in…\n\n**Figure 5.**\n\nSpatial analyses of VP differences between iAMDRPD and iAMDno\\_RPD eyes in the choriocapillaris. Gridwise VP differences (%) are plotted as a topographical map (**A**), and they were assessed according to the ETDRS sectors (**B**), eccentricity from the fovea (**C**), and by degrees around the macula (from −180° temporal to 0° nasal to 180° temporal) (**D**). Significance is denoted by \\**P* < 0.05 and \\*\\**P* < 0.01.\n\n[See this image and copyright information in PMC](#)\n\n## References\n\n1.  1.  Wightman AJ, Guymer RH.. Reticular pseudodrusen: current understanding. *Clin Exp Optom*. 2019; 102: 455–462. - [PubMed](/30298528/)\n2.  1.  Spaide RF, Ooto S, Curcio CA.. Subretinal drusenoid deposits AKA pseudodrusen. *Surv Ophthalmol*. 2018; 63: 782–815. - [PubMed](/29859199/)\n3.  1.  Wu Z, Fletcher EL, Kumar H, Greferath U, Guymer RH.. Reticular pseudodrusen: a critical phenotype in age-related macular degeneration. *Prog Retin Eye Res*. 2022; 88: 101017. - [PubMed](/34752916/)\n4.  1.  Trinh M, Cheung R, Duong A, Nivison-Smith L, Ly A.. OCT prognostic biomarkers for progression to late age-related macular degeneration: a systematic review and meta-analysis \\[published online ahead of print December 27, 2023\\]. *Ophthalmol Retina*, 10.1016/j.oret.2023.12.006. - [DOI](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.oret.2023.12.006) - [PubMed](/38154619/)\n5.  1.  Rosa R, Corazza P, Musolino M, et al.. Choroidal changes in intermediate age-related macular degeneration patients with drusen or pseudodrusen. *Eur J Ophthalmol*. 2021; 31: 505–513. - [PubMed](/32338527/)\n\nShow all 71 references\n\n## MeSH terms\n\n-   Cardiovascular Diseases\\*\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Cardiovascular+Diseases%22%5BMAJR%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n    -   [Search in MeSH](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh?term=Cardiovascular+Diseases)\n    -   [Add to Search](#)\n    \n-   Cross-Sectional Studies\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Cross-Sectional+Studies%22%5BMeSH%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n    -   [Search in MeSH](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh?term=Cross-Sectional+Studies)\n    -   [Add to Search](#)\n    \n-   Diabetic Retinopathy\\*\n    \n    Actions\n    \n    -   [Search in PubMed](/?term=%22Diabetic+Retinopathy%22%5BMAJR%5D&sort=date&sort_order=desc)\n    -   [Search in MeSH](https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/mesh?term=Diabetic+Retinopathy)\n    -   [Add to Search](#)\n    \n-   Humans\n